// Short-response source exam practice. Shown as flashcards: say/write your answer, then flip to compare.
// Data quoted is from the class sources (McCrindle 2025 infographic, ABS clock, Stile notes, West End profile).
export const HASS_EXAM_ANSWERS = [
  ['Describe the distribution of Australia\'s population shown on a population density map. (3 marks)', 'Australia\'s population is very unevenly distributed. It is concentrated along the coast, especially the east and south-east coast from Brisbane to Adelaide, and in the south-west around Perth, where densities are 10 or more people/km². About 85% of Australians live within 50 km of the coast. In contrast, the interior is sparsely populated, with less than 1 person/km² (the NT averages about 0.2 people/km², compared with the national average of 3.6).'],
  ['Explain two reasons why most Australians live near the coast. (4 marks)', '1. Physical – climate and rainfall: the Great Dividing Range forces moist Pacific winds to rise and drop rain on the eastern side, so the coast has a mild climate, reliable water and fertile soil for farming, while the interior is hot, dry desert. 2. Human – history and ports: European settlers arrived by ship, so capital cities like Sydney and Brisbane grew at harbours and river mouths as ports. Jobs, services and transport links became concentrated there, which pulled more people to the coastal cities.'],
  ['IPQE: Using the infographic, analyse state population growth rates: NSW 1.4%, VIC 2.1%, QLD 2.0%, WA 2.5%, SA 1.2%, TAS 0.3%, ACT 1.5%, NT 0.7%. (4 marks)', 'Identify: The data shows the annual population growth rate of each Australian state and territory (McCrindle, 2025). Pattern: Growth is fastest in WA, VIC and QLD and slowest in the smaller southern and northern areas. Quantify: WA grows fastest at 2.5% a year, followed by VIC at 2.1% and QLD at 2.0%, while TAS grows only 0.3% and NT 0.7%; the national rate is 1.8%. Exception: NSW is the most populous state (8.6 million, 31%) but grows only 1.4%, below the national average.'],
  ['Using the data, explain how Australia\'s population can grow at 1.8% a year when the fertility rate is only 1.5. (3 marks)', 'A fertility rate of 1.5 is below the 2.1 replacement level, so natural increase alone would not keep the population growing much. However, the ABS population clock shows one person arrives to live in Australia every 58 seconds, while only one resident leaves every 1 minute 58 seconds. This high net overseas migration adds more people than births minus deaths (a birth every 1 min 48 s vs a death every 2 min 50 s), so migration drives most of Australia\'s growth.'],
  ['Compare Australia\'s population pyramid for 1981 with 2026. Describe two significant changes and explain them. (4 marks)', '1. The base is narrower in 2026: there is a smaller share of children because the fertility rate has fallen to about 1.5 (the lowest ever recorded, compared with 3.5 in the baby boom). 2. The top is much wider in 2026: there are many more people aged 65+ because life expectancy has risen (81.1 for males, 85.1 for females) and the Baby Boomers are now 61–79. Overall the pyramid has changed from a triangle to a column shape, showing an ageing population; the median age is now 38.3.'],
  ['Explain one consequence of Australia\'s ageing population. (2 marks)', 'As the population ages, there are fewer workers for each retiree. For example, Baby Boomers are 18% of the population but only 12% of the workforce, and will be 0% by 2035. This means more demand for health care, aged care and pensions, paid for by a smaller share of workers, so Australia relies on migration to fill job shortages.'],
  ['Explain why Australia\'s centre of population has moved north and west over time. (2 marks)', 'The centre of population is the balance point of where everyone lives. It has moved north and west because Queensland and Western Australia have grown faster than the south-eastern states (WA 2.5% and QLD 2.0% a year vs TAS 0.3% and SA 1.2%), driven by jobs in mining and services, warmer climates and internal migration to South East Queensland and Perth.'],
  ['Using the maps, identify one similarity and one difference between the population distributions of Australia and the USA. (4 marks)', 'Similarity: both countries have dense populations along their east coasts and large sparsely populated dry areas (Australia\'s outback and the deserts and mountains of the western USA), and both are over 80% urban. Difference: Australia\'s population is concentrated on the coast in a few capital cities, while the USA\'s population is spread more widely, with many large inland cities such as Chicago, Dallas and Denver. The USA averages about 37 people/km² compared with Australia\'s 3.6, and has a megacity (New York, about 20 million), which Australia does not.'],
  ['Explain why the USA has more large inland cities than Australia. (3 marks)', 'The USA\'s interior has reliable rainfall, fertile soils and large navigable rivers such as the Mississippi, which supported farming and settlement, while Australia\'s interior is hot, dry desert. During the Industrial Revolution, American factories were built near coal, iron ore and railways, so industrial cities like Chicago and Pittsburgh grew inland and pulled workers from farms. Australia had fewer inland resources and railways, so its cities stayed near coastal ports.'],
  ['Explain two push and two pull factors for internal migration to South East Queensland. (4 marks)', 'Push factors from Sydney and Melbourne: very high house prices and cost of living, and long commutes and traffic congestion. Pull factors to SE Queensland: a warmer climate and coastal lifestyle, and cheaper housing with growing job opportunities. As a result, Queensland gains more people from interstate migration than any other state, and areas like Baringa–Caloundra West on the Sunshine Coast are growing rapidly.'],
  ['Explain one social, one environmental and one economic consequence of rapid population growth on the Sunshine Coast (e.g. Baringa–Caloundra West). (3 marks)', 'Social: new residents need schools, health services and shops, which may not be built fast enough, causing crowding. Environmental: bushland and farmland are cleared for new housing estates (urban sprawl), and more cars increase traffic and pollution. Economic: building houses and services creates construction and retail jobs, but strong demand pushes up house prices and rents for locals.'],
  ['Explain the causes and consequences of internal migration in China. (4 marks)', 'Causes: hundreds of millions of people have moved from the poorer rural west and centre to the east coast cities, pushed by low farm incomes and few jobs and pulled by factory and construction jobs with higher wages in cities like Shenzhen and Shanghai. Consequences: China went from about 20% urban in 1980 to over 65% today, creating megacities with pollution, congestion and expensive housing. Because of the hukou registration system, many migrant workers cannot easily access city schools and health care, and many children are left behind with grandparents in villages.'],
  ['Describe how Shenzhen has changed and explain why. (3 marks)', 'In 1979 Shenzhen was a group of fishing villages with about 30,000 people. In 1980 it became China\'s first Special Economic Zone, offering low taxes and special rules that attracted foreign companies and factories. Millions of rural migrants moved there for jobs, and it is now a technology megacity with over 17 million people, one of the fastest-growing cities in history.'],
  ['Explain why slums form in Indian cities and describe two challenges for people who live in them. (3 marks)', 'Slums form because rural migrants, pushed by poverty and drought and pulled by jobs, arrive in cities like Mumbai faster than affordable housing and infrastructure can be built, so they build informal shelters. Challenges include a lack of clean water and sanitation, which spreads disease, and severe overcrowding (Dharavi has about 1 million people in about 2 km²), which increases fire risk. However, Dharavi also has a busy informal economy in recycling, pottery and leather.'],
  ['Using the Southbank case study, explain how urban renewal has made Brisbane more liveable. Refer to social, environmental and economic benefits. (4 marks)', 'Before renewal, Southbank was an under-used industrial waterfront of wharves, warehouses and factories. After World Expo 88 drew millions of visitors, the Queensland government kept the land for public use and opened the 17-hectare South Bank Parklands in 1992. Socially, it gives residents free public space, recreation and culture (the Arbour, Queensland College of Art, cinemas), improving health and wellbeing. Environmentally, derelict industrial land became green parkland linked by the Goodwill Bridge for walking and cycling. Economically, it attracts tourists and events to the Brisbane Convention and Exhibition Centre, creating jobs and investment.'],
  ['Explain the three critical factors that make a city liveable. (3 marks)', '1. Residents feel safe, socially connected and included, which supports wellbeing. 2. Environmental sustainability, such as green space and lower pollution. 3. Access to affordable, diverse housing linked by public transport, walking and cycling to jobs, education, shops, parks, health services, leisure and culture. A city is only truly liveable if these apply to all residents, not just the wealthy inner city.'],
  ['Explain how West End has changed over time. (4 marks)', 'West End, known as Kurilpa ("place of water rats"), was rainforest cleared for farming from the 1840s. In the late 1800s and 1900s river access made it an industrial, working-class suburb with gas works and factories and cheap housing near the CBD. After WW2 Greek migrants settled there ("Little Athens", 75% of Brisbane\'s Greeks by 1980), followed by Vietnamese migrants. Gentrification then occurred in waves: students and artists, then home renovators, and since the 2000s developers building apartments (e.g. West Village). Today a 3-bedroom house costs about $1.75 million and about 60% of residents rent.'],
  ['Explain why there is a significant Greek and Vietnamese population in West End. Refer to push and pull factors. (3 marks)', 'Greek migrants were pushed by poverty, unemployment and civil war after World War 2 and pulled by jobs in Australia\'s post-war boom. Vietnamese refugees were pushed by war and persecution after 1975 and pulled by safety and resettlement. Both groups settled in West End because it had cheap housing, factory jobs and was close to the CBD, and once a community formed (by 1980, 75% of Brisbane\'s Greeks lived there), family and cultural ties pulled more migrants to the same area.'],
  ['Explain the positive and negative consequences of gentrification in West End for two different stakeholders. (4 marks)', 'Long-term low-income renters: positive – safer streets, repaired buildings and better services; negative – rising rents (from about $620 to over $1,300 a week) can force them to leave (displacement), and projects like the Dudley Street proposal would replace 14 cheaper apartments with 8 luxury ones. Developers and new wealthy residents: positive – developers profit and new residents gain modern housing close to the CBD, transport and cafes; negative – they face community opposition and conflict, such as the 2021 Kuripla Garden protest by Growing Forward. Because some gain and others lose, gentrification is contested.'],
  ['Describe the Kuripla Garden protest: who, where and why. (3 marks)', 'Who: the community group Growing Forward, supported by Councillor Jonathan Sri, protested against developer Bluebird Property. Where: Kuripla Garden at 250 Boundary Street, Highgate Hill (West End), next to the development site at 5 Dudley Street. Why: the developer proposed demolishing 14 affordable 2-bedroom apartments and building 8 luxury 3-bedroom apartments that would house fewer people and affect the garden, which gives free food to people facing food insecurity. Protesters saw it as part of the "mass gentrification" of West End.'],
];

export const HASS_KEYS = Object.keys(HASS_TOPICS);
export const HASS_LESSONS = Object.fromEntries(HASS_KEYS.map((k) => [k, HASS_TOPICS[k].lesson]));
export const HASS_TRICKS = Object.fromEntries(HASS_KEYS.map((k) => [k, { tips: HASS_TOPICS[k].tips, cards: HASS_TOPICS[k].cards }]));
